// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RCFFUTA FYB Dinner — Award Nomination & Voting Standard
//
// THIS FILE IS THE AWARD STANDARD. Editing it changes what the fellowship
// recognises, what each award means, and what a nominee must satisfy before
// they can appear on a ballot.
//
// It lives here, in the repository, and not in the database on purpose. The
// criteria are a governance document owned by the Screening Committee, not a
// settings field: if an admin could rewrite a checklist at 11pm the night
// before voting, the checklist would stop being a guarantee and become a
// formality. Changing criteria should take a review and a deploy.
//
// ── ADDING AN AWARD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Add an object to "categories" below. Every field marked required must be
// filled. Then, in the admin dashboard, create the category — it will appear
// in the picker. That order is enforced: a category whose slug does not match
// a "key" here is dropped from the ballot and blocks voting from opening.
// No criteria, no ballot.
//
// ── FIELD REFERENCE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   key           required  URL-safe id. Becomes the category's slug. Never
//                           change it once a category exists — it is the only
//                           link between a live ballot and these criteria.
//   title         required  What voters read as the award's name.
//   entryKind     required  "individual" | "clique" | "brand"
//                             individual — one registered finalist, one face
//                             clique     — a named friend group, 3+ members
//                             brand      — a business: name, logo, founders
//   gender        required  "any" | "male" | "female". Admin-side check only;
//                           it never changes what a voter sees.
//   blurb         required  One line, shown under the title on the ballot.
//   definition    required  What the award actually recognises.
//   disqualifiers required  The "does NOT qualify on its own" list. These are
//                           the false signals that make awards feel rigged, so
//                           name them plainly.
//   checklist     required  Every box a nominee must clear at screening. Each
//                           needs a specific named instance — what happened,
//                           when, and who can confirm it.
//   note          optional  { title, body } — an extra test worth spelling
//                           out, e.g. the clique "No Camera" test.
//
// Comments are allowed anywhere (// and /* */), and trailing commas are fine.
// Source document: .temp/RCFFUTA-FYB-Award-Standard.md — keep the two in step.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    // The sentence that settles every close call. Shown at the top of the
    // public standard page and the admin criteria panel.
    "principle": "Voting determines the winner among qualified nominees. Voting never determines whether someone qualifies. If a nominee cannot satisfy their category's checklist, they do not appear on the ballot — no exceptions.",

    // Apply to every category, in the order they should be read.
    "generalRules": [
        "Only active fellowship members can nominate or vote. One account, one person, one vote per category.",
        "No self-nomination. Every nomination comes from at least 2 members who are not the nominee.",
        "One win per person. Nobody takes home two categories.",
        "Evidence over assumption. \"Everybody knows\" is never enough — every checklist item needs a specific named instance: what happened, when, and who can confirm it.",
        "A Screening Committee of 3–5 people checks each nominee against their category's checklist, item by item, before voting opens. Every box, or no ballot.",
        "One written appeal per disqualified nomination, within a fixed window, before voting opens.",
        "No vote buying, no incentives, no multiple accounts. Violations can disqualify the beneficiary even after votes are counted.",
        "A nominee found to have used manipulation, pressure, exclusion, or unkindness to win forfeits the award, even after voting."
    ],

    // The three stages, in order. Voting is the last one, never the first.
    "screeningStages": [
        {
            "title": "Nominate",
            "body": "Members put names forward with evidence — specific instances, dated, with someone who can confirm each one."
        },
        {
            "title": "Screen",
            "body": "The Committee clears or disqualifies each nomination against the category checklist, item by item. Decisions are documented."
        },
        {
            "title": "Vote",
            "body": "Only screened nominees reach the ballot. Voting decides who wins among qualified people — it never decides who qualifies."
        }
    ],

    // Terms the checklists lean on, so nobody has to guess what they mean.
    "definitions": [
        {
            "term": "Active fellowship member",
            "meaning": "A registered member who has attended and participated in fellowship activities during the current academic session."
        },
        {
            "term": "Academic session",
            "meaning": "One full university session — typically two semesters, per FUTA's calendar."
        },
        {
            "term": "Exco",
            "meaning": "A member currently holding an Executive office — President, Vice President, Secretary, and other officially recognized Executive positions."
        },
        {
            "term": "Worker",
            "meaning": "A member serving in a fellowship unit (Bible Study, Prayer, Choir, Media, Protocol, Ushering, Follow-up, and so on) who does not hold an Exco office."
        },
        {
            "term": "Non-worker",
            "meaning": "A member not currently serving in any fellowship unit."
        },
        {
            "term": "Corroboration",
            "meaning": "Confirmation of a claimed instance by someone other than the nominator and the nominee."
        }
    ],

    // ── The awards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Order here is the order they are offered in the admin picker and listed
    // on the public standard page. It does not control ballot order — admin
    // arranges that.
    "categories": [
        {
            "key": "most-versatile",
            "title": "Most Versatile",
            "entryKind": "individual",
            "gender": "any",
            "blurb": "Real competence in several different arenas — not just a full calendar.",
            "definition": "Demonstrates genuine competence across multiple, clearly different areas of fellowship and school life — not just being busy or involved in many things.",
            "disqualifiers": [
                "Being involved in many activities without demonstrated skill in them",
                "Being generally \"busy\"",
                "Being popular across different groups"
            ],
            "checklist": [
                "Active member this session",
                "Named competence shown in 3 or more distinct areas — academics, ministry, sports, creative work, leadership",
                "At least one specific, dated example per area",
                "Corroborated by 2+ members not from the same friend group",
                "Sustained involvement in each area, not a one-off appearance"
            ]
        },
        {
            "key": "most-resourceful",
            "title": "Most Resourceful",
            "entryKind": "individual",
            "gender": "any",
            "blurb": "Finds a way. Repeatedly, for other people, usually with very little.",
            "definition": "Consistently finds practical solutions to real problems for others or the fellowship, often with limited means.",
            "disqualifiers": [
                "A general reputation as \"smart\" or \"capable\"",
                "One-time help with no pattern behind it",
                "Having money or connections without personally solving a problem"
            ],
            "checklist": [
                "Active member this session",
                "2 or more specific instances of solving a real problem, each named with date and context",
                "Who benefited from each instance is identified",
                "Corroborated by someone who witnessed or benefited",
                "Shows a repeated pattern, not a single incident"
            ]
        },
        {
            "key": "most-fashionable-male",
            "title": "Most Fashionable (Male)",
            "entryKind": "individual",
            "gender": "male",
            "blurb": "Consistent style across many occasions — not one very good Sunday.",
            "definition": "Consistent, well-put-together personal style and grooming across multiple occasions — not one outfit.",
            "disqualifiers": [
                "One standout outfit at one event",
                "Expensive clothing without a consistent styling sense",
                "General popularity"
            ],
            "checklist": [
                "Active member this session",
                "Style observed on 3 or more separate, named occasions",
                "Reflects personal effort and taste, not a one-off borrowed or rented outfit",
                "Recognized by people outside the nominee's close circle",
                "Nominator names the specific occasions and what stood out"
            ]
        },
        {
            "key": "most-fashionable-female",
            "title": "Most Fashionable (Female)",
